  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nNo significant changes to the extended forecast. The upper-level \nridge retrogrades westward over the Desert Southwest late this week, \nbut our forecast area will still remain under the eastern influence \nof this feature. Weak shortwave impulses embedded along the eastern \nperiphery of the ridge may pivot over southeast New Mexico and the \nPermian Basin Thursday afternoon and evening, while a weak surface \nboundary also sags toward northern portions of our area. Isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms (10-40%) may subsequently \ndevelop over these locations late Thursday afternoon into Thursday \nevening, with the best chances favored from Lea County into the \nnorthern Permian Basin and western Low Rolling Plains. Other \nisolated to scattered (20-50%) showers and storms will also develop \nover the higher terrain areas of the western CWA Thursday \nafternoon/evening. Upper-level ridging will tend to build back over \nwest Texas and southeast New Mexico Friday through the weekend, \nbefore shifting eastward toward the Gulf Coast states Monday. \nIsolated to scattered showers and storms will be possible over the \nhigher terrain each day. There may also be low chances for \nconvection over southeast New Mexico and portions of the Permian \nBasin this weekend into early next week, though coverage will be \ndependent on the strength and placement of the ridge. The best \nchances should remain confined to our western CWA through the \nextended period. Temperatures remain above normal each day with \nhighs mostly in the mid 90s to around 103 degrees. Friday and \nSaturday appear to be the hottest days of the extended period.",
